    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S6988A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to real estate\nlicense terms and service with a real estate broker\n\nPURPOSE:\nThe bill amends the real property law to make a technical amendment to\nthe real estate license term and expiration, and streamlines the process\nby which real estate brokers and salespersons terminate and establish\nnew records of association.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSubdivision 2 of section 441-a of the real property law is amended to\nmake the \"Term\" of a real estate license the two year term up to and\nincluding the date specified on the real estate license.\n\nSubdivision 11 of section 441-a of the real property law is amended to\nrequire a real estate broker to the termination of association with the\nSecretary of State if a real estate salesperson leaves the service of\nthe real estate broker to pay a $20 fee to the Department of State upon\nthe establishment of a new record of association with a broker.\n\nSection 2 This act shall take effect on January 1, 2013.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nReal estate practitioners are licensed in New York by the Department of\nState for a period of two years beginning at the application approval\ndate. Subdivision 2 of Section 441-a of the real property law relating\nto real estate license terms however still refers to a universal license\nexpiration date of the \"thirty-first day of October of the year in which\nthe license by its terms expires.\" This bill provides a technical amend-\nment to bring the text of subdivision 2 of Section 441-a of the Real\nProperty Law in line with the actual licensing renewal in practice in\nNew York State.\n\nSubdivision 11 of Section 441-a of the Real Property Law currently\nrequires real estate brokers to file a termination of association with\nthe Secretary of State and pay a $10 fee when a real estate salesperson\ndisassociates with their brokerage. The real estate salesperson or spon-\nsoring broker is then also required to pay a $10 fee when the salesper-\nson re-associates or joins with a new broker. The disassociation fees\nprovide an opportunity for contention and therefore could delay the real\nestate salesperson from actively participating under their license. This\nbill would amend Subdivision 11 of Section 441-a of the Real Property\nLaw to streamline this process by requiring a real estate broker to file\nonly the termination of association with the Secretary of State when a\nreal estate salesperson leaves the service of the real estate broker,\nand requiring the salesperson or their new sponsoring broker, to pay a\n$20 fee upon the establishment of a new record of association with the\ndepartment of state.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective January 1, 2013.\n\n",
